      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">President Trump has ordered Lisa Cook, a governor of the Federal Reserve, to a closed hearing at the White House on November 5, where three officials he named will weigh allegations that she made false statements on mortgages in 2021.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="I hereby establish a committee of inquiry (Committee) to investigate allegations that Federal Reserve Governor Lisa D. Cook made false statements in connection with one or more mortgage instruments." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="conduct an in-person hearing to consider the evidence and allegations against Governor Cook" /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The hearing shall be closed to the public but shall be transcribed." /> A president can fire a Fed governor only "for cause," and the three will recommend whether he has it.<Cite s="usc-12-242" passage="unless sooner removed for cause by the President" /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="shall recommend whether there is cause to remove Governor Cook from her position on the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">This is his second try. Last year he sent her a letter firing her, and in June the Supreme Court kept her in office because he had not given her a chance to answer the charges, while leaving him free to try again.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President’s letter of August 25" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President failed to afford Cook the procedural protections to which she was entitled by statute" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="but not to forbid the President from trying again, if he chooses to do so" /> If he succeeds, he gets to fill her seat on the Fed's seven-member board, whose governors vote on interest rates alongside five presidents of regional Fed banks.<Cite s="fed-board-members" passage="The seven members of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System are nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate." /><Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="As a member of the board of governors, Cook is also one of the 12 voting members of the committee." /> Three of the seven, including the chair, Kevin Warsh, are already his appointees, and his pick for a fourth seat would need Senate confirmation.<Cite s="reuters-fed-seats-2026-08-26" passage="Besides Warsh, Trump would have only two of his own appointees to choose from: Michelle Bowman, who is already the Fed’s vice chair for supervision, and Fed Governor Christopher Waller" /><Cite s="fed-warsh-oath-2026-05-22" passage="President Donald J. Trump nominated Mr. Warsh on March 4, 2026." /><Cite s="fed-board-members" passage="The seven members of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System are nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Cook denies wrongdoing and has not been charged with a crime.<Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="Cook has denied any wrongdoing and has not been charged with a crime." /> Her lawyers have not said whether she will go; first, they say, they want to judge whether the hearing is "a genuine one and not simply a box checking exercise."<Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="said they were considering having Cook appear at the Nov. 5 hearing" /><Cite s="lowell-eisen-statement-2026-10-09" passage="We have engaged with the White House to evaluate whether the process proposed has a possibility of being a genuine one and not simply a box checking exercise." /> Whatever the committee recommends, the decision is Trump's.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="shall recommend whether there is cause to remove Governor Cook from her position on the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve" /><Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="There is no set deadline for that final decision." /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-mortgages">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who Cook is, and the charge against her</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook taught economics at Michigan State University until President Biden put her on the Fed's board, which she joined in May 2022 as its first Black woman.<Cite s="fed-cook-bio" passage="Dr. Cook was a professor of economics and international relations at Michigan State University" /><Cite s="fed-cook-bio" passage="took office as a member of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System on May 23, 2022" /><Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="Cook was appointed by former President Joe Biden and is the first Black woman on the Fed’s board." /> He reappointed her in 2023, to a term that runs until January 2038.<Cite s="fed-cook-bio" passage="for a term ending January 31, 2038" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The charge reaches back to the summer of 2021, before she joined the Fed, when she took out two mortgages two weeks apart: one on a house in Ann Arbor, Michigan, the other on a condo in Atlanta.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="Just two weeks later, you signed another document for a property in Georgia stating that it would be your primary residence for that same year." /> The White House says she promised on each that the home would be her main residence that year.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="Just two weeks later, you signed another document for a property in Georgia stating that it would be your primary residence for that same year." /> Its letter says those promises "benefited you by decreasing your interest rates," since loans on second homes typically cost 0.25 to 0.50 percentage points more.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="Doing so benefited you by decreasing your interest rates, as interest rates on secondary residence mortgages are typically between 0.25-0.50% higher than their primary residence counterparts" /> It also says the Atlanta condo was listed for rent in September 2022.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="the Atlanta address was then listed for rent in September 2022" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The accusation came from Bill Pulte, who runs the Federal Housing Finance Agency, in a criminal referral he sent the Justice Department on August 15, 2025.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="On August 15, 2025, Director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency William J. Pulte submitted a criminal referral to the Department of Justice" /> Three weeks later AP, citing one person familiar with the matter, reported that investigators had issued subpoenas in an inquiry into the two homes.<Cite s="ap-doj-probe-2025-09-04" passage="Investigators have issued subpoenas as part of an inquiry into Cook that was spawned by a criminal referral from the country’s top housing regulator" /> The department would not comment, and more than a year later no charge has followed.<Cite s="ap-doj-probe-2025-09-04" passage="A Justice Department spokesperson declined to comment on the probe" /><Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="has not been charged with a crime" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook's lawyers call her signing of the Atlanta mortgage as her primary residence "an entirely inadvertent oversight," and say an oversight is not fraud.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Governor Cook’s signing of a “primary” mortgage agreement" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="was an entirely inadvertent oversight" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="An inadvertent oversight is not fraudulent or criminal." /> She did list the condo for rent, they say, but only briefly, and decided not to rent it.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="though Governor Cook briefly listed the property for rent, she ultimately decided not to rent it" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="why-now">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why a hearing now</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump's first attempt came within days of the referral going public.<Cite s="dcd-cook-opinion-2025-09-09" passage="Within thirty minutes, President Trump posted on the social media platform Truth Social that “Cook must resign, now!!!,”" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President’s letter of August 25" /> On August 20, 2025, Pulte posted pages of it; within thirty minutes Trump posted "Cook must resign, now!!!" and five days later he sent her the letter firing her.<Cite s="dcd-cook-opinion-2025-09-09" passage="Within thirty minutes, President Trump posted on the social media platform Truth Social that “Cook must resign, now!!!,”" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President’s letter of August 25" /> Cook sued, and a federal judge, Jia Cobb, kept her in her job while the case went on.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="After Cook filed suit, the District Court issued a preliminary injunction to prevent her removal." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The law that protects her seat gives governors 14-year terms and never defines the cause a president needs to cut one short.<Cite s="usc-12-242" passage="each member shall hold office for a term of fourteen years from the expiration of the term of his predecessor, unless sooner removed for cause by the President" /> On June 29 the Supreme Court, 5 to 4, kept Cook in office, holding that she was owed "some explanation of the evidence at issue, some avenue for a response, and a deadline by which a response would be due," and had been given none.<Cite s="fox-cook-2026-10-09" passage="In a 5-4 ruling, the court ruled she had not been given adequate time to respond to the allegations against her" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="At minimum, Cook was entitled to some explanation of the evidence at issue, some avenue for a response, and a deadline by which a response would be due." /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President failed to afford Cook the procedural protections to which she was entitled by statute" /> It left aside whether she had done anything wrong, and said Trump could try again.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="In this opinion, we have not addressed the facts" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="but not to forbid the President from trying again, if he chooses to do so" /> That chance to respond, the Court added, could come "on written materials only," with no hearing in person.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="We see no reason why that opportunity “may not be had on written materials only,” with no oral presentation." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump tried again on paper first. On August 5 the White House sent Cook notice that he was considering removing her, along with its evidence and 21 days to answer, and her lawyers answered on August 26.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="you are hereby provided notice that the President is considering removing you from your position on the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve" /><Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="Please provide a written response with your explanation for your false statements and accompanying evidence within 21 days." /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Response by Governor Lisa D. Cook to August 5, 2026, Notice of Potential Removal" /> Six weeks later, on October 7, he signed the memo that adds the committee and a hearing in person, the step the Court said he could do without.<Cite s="fox-cook-2026-10-09" passage="according to a notice dated Wednesday, Oct. 7, 2026, but released on Friday by the Trump administration" /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="on November 5, 2026, conduct an in-person hearing" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="We see no reason why that opportunity “may not be had on written materials only,” with no oral presentation." /> The memo does not mention her lawyers' answer, or say why the written exchange was not enough.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="the Committee shall investigate the allegations against Governor Cook" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The three officials who will hear her are Kevin Hassett, Trump's chief economic adviser; Andrea Lucas, who chairs the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission; and Keith Sonderling, the labor secretary and acting head of the Office of Government Ethics.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The Assistant to the President for Economic Policy, the Chairman of the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, and the Director of the Office of Government Ethics shall serve as members of the Committee." /><Cite s="reuters-cook-inquiry-2026-10-09" passage="Trump's chief economic policy advisor Kevin Hassett, Equal Employment Opportunity Commission chair Andrea Lucas, and acting Office of Government Ethics director Keith Sonderling will comprise the newly formed committee" /><Cite s="wsj-cook-hearing-2026-10-09" passage="the ethics office’s current acting director, Keith Sonderling, who also serves as labor secretary" /> For up to four hours they, or Justice Department lawyers they choose, can question her.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The hearing shall occur at the White House and shall last no longer than 4 hours." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="it may authorize its representatives, including Department of Justice personnel, to conduct that questioning" /> She can bring a lawyer and offer argument, written evidence and written statements from witnesses.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="Governor Cook shall appear at the hearing and be examined by the Committee, its representatives, and her legal counsel, if any." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="Governor Cook may present argument, written evidence, and written witness statements regarding the evidence and allegations." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" kind="record" detail>
          <figure class="story-figure">
            <figcaption><strong>From the mortgages to the hearing</strong></figcaption>
            <DataTable columns={["Date", "What happened"]} rows={chronology.rows} ariaLabel="Dates from Cook's 2021 mortgages to the November 5 hearing" />
          </figure>
          <p>Cook sued on August 28, 2025, and Judge Jia Cobb kept her in office on September 9.<Cite s="dcd-cook-opinion-2025-09-09" passage="Cook filed a six-count complaint on August 28, 2025." /> The Supreme Court heard the case on January 21.<Cite s="scotus-oral-argument-2026-01-21" passage="Deceit or gross negligence by a" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Whether a mortgage form is cause to fire her</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Neither side disputes that she signed both forms, so the fight is over whether those signatures amount to cause.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Governor Cook’s signing of a “primary” mortgage agreement" /> The Supreme Court has already settled that she gets a chance to answer before any firing.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="Only after Cook has had the opportunity to respond to the charges made against her" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Why Trump says it is</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump frames it as a matter of honesty: his memo says it is his job to fire "subordinates who cannot be trusted to tell the truth and follow the law."<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="As President, it is my job to ensure the laws are faithfully executed, including by firing subordinates who cannot be trusted to tell the truth and follow the law." /> His lawyers told the Supreme Court that deceit or gross negligence by a financial regulator in her own financial dealings is cause.<Cite s="scotus-oral-argument-2026-01-21" passage="Deceit or gross negligence by a" /><Cite s="scotus-oral-argument-2026-01-21" passage="financial regulator in financial transactions" /> And the White House's August letter says she cannot have meant both promises: it was "impossible that you intended to honor both."<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="The President believes it is inconceivable that you were not aware of your first commitment when making the second and impossible that you intended to honor both." /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Why Cook says it is not</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Her lawyers say the Atlanta form was an honest mistake, made in her private life before she joined the Fed.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="The pre-office conduct at issue, all of which took place in Governor Cook’s private capacity" /> They also say neither the White House's letter nor Pulte's referral declares that she actually got a lower rate on any property.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="neither the Letter nor Director Pulte’s Referral declares that Governor Cook actually received a preferential interest rate on any property" /> The letter does say the promises lowered her rates, but its only figure is the typical gap between loans on main homes and second homes, not the rate on her own loans.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="Doing so benefited you by decreasing your interest rates, as interest rates on secondary residence mortgages are typically between 0.25-0.50% higher than their primary residence counterparts" /> By their account, she started out by asking the lender's website about a "vacation home" mortgage.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="began with Governor Cook using the lender’s website to seek information about a “vacation home” mortgage" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A printout from that website, dated May 28, 2021, lists the Atlanta condo's use as "Vacation Home," and her lawyers say it went to the same lender.<Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Bank-Fund Staff Federal Credit Union - Rate Search Results" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Property Use: Vacation Home" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="A separate, contemporaneous May 28, 2021 loan document provided to the same lender clearly states the Atlanta condo’s “Property Use” as a “Vacation Home.”" /> But it is a rate quote for a 15-year loan that warns the actual rate could be higher, and the Atlanta mortgage she signed in July ran 30 years.<Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Conforming 15 Yr Fixed" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Your actual rate, payment, and costs could be higher. Get an official Loan Estimate before choosing a loan." /><Cite s="reuters-cook-mortgages-factbox-2025-08-26" passage="Again the address is not listed but the value and term of the loan matches her $540,000 mortgage on her Atlanta, Georgia condominium." /> Documents AP obtained show that on a form for a security clearance she called the condo a "second home," and county tax records show she has never claimed Georgia's homestead exemption on it, the property-tax break for a primary residence.<Cite s="ap-vacation-home-2025-09-12" passage="Documents obtained by The Associated Press also showed that on a second form submitted by Cook to gain a security clearance, she described the property as a “second home.”" /><Cite s="ap-vacation-home-2025-09-12" passage="Fulton County tax records show Cook has never claimed a homestead exemption on the condo, which allows someone who uses a property as their primary residence to reduce their property taxes" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Her lawyers also say she is being singled out. Trump himself, they note, reportedly took out primary-residence mortgages on two Florida homes seven weeks apart in 1993.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="In 1993, President Trump reportedly acquired “primary” residence mortgages for two different Florida homes just seven weeks apart." /> Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and Attorney General Todd Blanche have done the same on more than one home, they say, yet Pulte has made no public referral against any of them.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and Attorney General Todd Blanche have both entered into “primary” residence mortgages for multiple homes." /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Director Pulte has made no public referrals against President Trump himself nor any of his cabinet members referred to in this response" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">What the courts have said</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Judge Cobb held last year that conduct from before a governor took office cannot, by itself, be cause, and the mortgages came a year before Cook joined the Fed.<Cite s="dcd-cook-opinion-2025-09-09" passage="“For cause” thus does not contemplate removing an individual purely for conduct that occurred before they began in office." /><Cite s="fed-cook-bio" passage="took office as a member of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System on May 23, 2022" /> The Supreme Court adopted neither her view nor the government's, and left that question open.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="The Court rejects both parties’ positions." /> Instead it set "a substantial threshold" for cause, to be judged by how serious the misconduct is and how closely it bears on the job.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="That counsels a substantial threshold for “cause.”" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="will depend, at least in part, on the seriousness of the alleged misconduct, and the extent of any nexus that may exist to the Governor’s professional duties" /> A court, it said, may also ask whether a firing is really an effort to install someone "more congenial."<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="or whether it simply represents an effort to secure a “more congenial” replacement" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Where interest rates come in</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government itself told the Supreme Court that "for cause" rules out removing a governor "for policy disagreement," while covering doubts about her integrity, the reason Trump gives.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="acknowledging that the “ ‘for cause’ provision rules out removal for no reason at all, or for policy disagreement”" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="That excludes a “mere policy disagreement,” according to the Government" /> On the evening of October 10 Trump gave two reasons for forming the committee: "because I think she's dishonest," and "she’s an automatic no vote, and that's not a good thing."<Cite s="doyle-trump-remarks-2026-10-10" passage="tonight that he formed a committee to investigate the allegations against Fed Governor Lisa Cook" /><Cite s="doyle-trump-remarks-2026-10-10" passage="because I think she's dishonest" /><Cite s="doyle-trump-remarks-2026-10-10" passage="And she’s an automatic no vote, and that's not a good thing." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Her record has no dissents: she has voted with the majority on every rate decision.<Cite s="reuters-cook-inquiry-2026-10-09" passage="She has always cast her vote with the majority on the Fed's policy-making committee" /> When the Fed raised rates 12 to 0 on September 16, she voted with Trump's three appointees, Warsh, Michelle Bowman and Christopher Waller.<Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-09-16" passage="Voting for this action: Kevin Warsh, John C. Williams, Michael S. Barr, Michelle W. Bowman, Lisa D. Cook, Beth M. Hammack, Philip N. Jefferson, Neel Kashkari, Lorie K. Logan, Anna Paulson, Jerome H. Powell, and Christopher J. Waller." /> Trump answered that rates "should be 1%, or less."<Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The hearing itself</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook's lawyers say she welcomes the chance to "present the facts so she can clear her name," but that Trump's "repeated statements that he has already decided to fire" her leave them doubting the hearing is genuine.<Cite s="lowell-eisen-statement-2026-10-09" passage="Governor Cook welcomes the opportunity to present the facts so she can clear her name and demonstrate there is no legal basis to fire her." /><Cite s="lowell-eisen-statement-2026-10-09" passage="In light of the President’s repeated statements that he has already decided to fire Governor Cook" /><Cite s="lowell-eisen-statement-2026-10-09" passage="We have engaged with the White House to evaluate whether the process proposed has a possibility of being a genuine one and not simply a box checking exercise." /> They single out Hassett, who said on August 27, 2025, two days after Trump's letter firing her, that she should take leave from the Fed during the legal fight.<Cite s="wtimes-hassett-2025-08-27" passage="so she should take leave from the Fed during the looming legal fight" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President’s letter of August 25" /> Abbe Lowell, one of her lawyers, said on CNN that the White House needs to explain why Hassett is on the committee, and that he does not know what role the Justice Department will play.<Cite s="cnn-lowell-2026-10-09" passage="What they need to do is explain why did they put Mr. Hassett on this committee." /><Cite s="cnn-lowell-2026-10-09" passage="I don't know what role that would be and why." /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>What the Supreme Court decided</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The Guardian wrote that the Supreme Court found her earlier firing "unconstitutional," and Mediaite that the Court said Trump "did not have the power to fire a member of the Federal Reserve."<Cite s="guardian-cook-2026-10-09" passage="despite a June supreme court ruling that her earlier firing was unconstitutional" /><Cite s="mediaite-cook-2026-10-09" passage="saying the commander in chief did not have the power to fire a member of the Federal Reserve" /> Both overstate the ruling: the Court kept Cook in office because Trump had not given her the notice and chance to respond that the law requires, set aside her constitutional argument, and said he could try again.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President failed to afford Cook the procedural protections to which she was entitled by statute" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="The Court need not address Cook’s constitutional due process argument" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="but not to forbid the President from trying again, if he chooses to do so" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>"A transcript would be published"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">AP's report, carried by PBS and others, said "a transcript would be published, the memo said," and the Guardian wrote that the hearing would be "transcribed and published."<Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="The hearing wouldn’t be public but a transcript would be published, the memo said." /><Cite s="guardian-cook-2026-10-09" passage="which will be closed to the public but transcribed and published" /> The memo says the hearing "shall be transcribed" and nothing about releasing the transcript.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The hearing shall be closed to the public but shall be transcribed." /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">Cook has until {computed.statementDue}, three days before the hearing, to send the committee a written statement if she chooses.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="provided the statement is received at least 3 days before the hearing" /> The day after the hearing, the two sides in her lawsuit over the first firing owe Judge Cobb a joint report on the case.<Cite s="cobb-minute-order-2026-09-22" passage="The Parties are ORDERED to confer and submit a joint status report by November 6, 2026." /> Cook then has until November 10 to send a second statement.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="Governor Cook may submit a post-hearing written statement to the Committee by November 10, 2026." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">After that the committee reports to Trump "as soon as practicable," and no deadline binds his decision.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="As soon as practicable after receipt of that statement" /><Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="There is no set deadline for that final decision." /> If he fires her, she can go back to court, and only then will judges weigh whether the charges amount to cause.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="At that point, Cook may seek judicial review under the framework described in this opinion" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="And only then can the courts assess the validity and sufficiency of such charges." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Until Trump decides, Cook is still a governor, with a vote at the Fed's next rate meeting on October 28.<Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="upcoming vote on interest rates scheduled for Oct. 28" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" kind="checked" detail>
          <p>The memo does not say whether Cook's answers at the hearing could be used against her in a criminal case.<Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="but it does not say whether her answers would be protected from use in a later criminal case" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

